[{"id":"0021","name":["Catherine Sharp"],"sortname":"Sharp Catherine","prog":["64","65","114","120","167"],"bio":"<p>Catherine Sharp is a conrunner and technical writer from Wales, who spent many years living in Northern Ireland and working on Irish conventions plus others including the Glasgow 2024 and LAcon V worldcons. She's currently Chair of Octocon!&nbsp;In her limited free time, she's an endurance athlete, cat wrangler, and occasional set painter.<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"R0001","name":["C.E. (Catie) Murphy"],"sortname":"Murphy C.E. (Catie)","prog":["64","65","67","74","75","79","99","111","113","131"],"bio":"<p>C.E. (Catie) Murphy was born and raised in Alaska, where she held the usual grab-bag of jobs usually seen in an authorial biography, including public library volunteer, archival assistant, cannery worker, and web designer. She has something in the region of eighty books out, under a startling variety of names and genres, ranging from fantasy and science fiction to cozy mysteries to paranormal romance, and probably some dabbling in other things that she&rsquo;s forgetting.<\/p>\n<p>Catie now lives with her family in her ancestral home of Ireland, a magical place where it rains a lot but nothing one could seriously regard as winter ever actually arrives. Her hobbies include making jam and fudge, and also&hellip;writing. She has been told she does not really understand the concept of &lsquo;hobby&rsquo;, and accepts that as very likely.<\/p>\n<p>She can be found online at <a href=\"https:\/\/catiemurphy.com\/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">https:\/\/catiemurphy.com\/<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/ppa7857fb3536a2aa680c88f2126a9fc31.png"}},{"id":"R0002","name":["Adrian Tchaikovsky"],"sortname":"Tchaikovsky Adrian","prog":["64","65","68","70","95","99","114","130"],"bio":"<p>Adrian Tchaikovsky is a British science-fiction and fantasy writer known for a wide-variety of work including the Children of Time, Final Architecture, Dogs of War, Tyrant Philosophers and Shadows of the Apt series, as well as standalone books such as <em>Elder Race<\/em>, <em>Doors of Eden<\/em>, <em>Spiderlight<\/em> and many others. <em>Children of Time<\/em> and its series has won the Arthur C Clarke and BSFA awards, and his other works have won the British Fantasy, British Science Fiction and Sidewise Awards.<\/p>\n<p>He can be found online at <a href=\"https:\/\/adriantchaikovsky.com\/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">https:\/\/adriantchaikovsky.com\/<\/a>.<\/p>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/pp1acd5997730de19ffd0b3be7694b81e6.png"}},{"id":"R0003","name":["Premee Mohamed"],"sortname":"Mohamed Premee","prog":["65","69","132","145","146","147"],"bio":"<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Premee Mohamed is a Nebula, World Fantasy, Ignyte, Locus, and Aurora award-winning Indo-Caribbean scientist and speculative fiction author based in Edmonton, Alberta. She has also been a finalist for the Hugo, British Fantasy, British Science Fiction, Ursula K. Le Guin, and Crawford awards. In 2024, she was the Edmonton Public Library writer-in-residence, and she has taught at establishments including the Banff Institute for the Arts, the Clarion Workshop, and the Carl Brandon Society. She has judged short fiction contests for CBC and Alberta Views Magazine and can be found on her website at <a href=\"http:\/\/www.premeemohamed.com\/\">www.premeemohamed.com<\/a><\/p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Find her at:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https:\/\/www.premeemohamed.com\">Author&rsquo;s Website<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"https:\/\/bsky.app\/profile\/premeemohamed.com\">Bluesky<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"https:\/\/www.instagram.com\/premeesaurus\/\">Instagram<\/a><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><em>(Author photo (c) LENSMAN STUDIO, Michael MENIANE<\/em>)<\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/ppa92c80b9f76312e625c43d58409fb222.png"}},{"id":"R0004","name":["Gareth Hanrahan"],"sortname":"Hanrahan Gareth","prog":["65","75","107","110","114","133"],"bio":"<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Gareth Hanrahan&rsquo;s a writer and game designer living in Cork. His gaming work includes Moria, the Darkening of Mirkwood, the Pirates of Drinax, the Dracula Dossier, Eyes of the Stone Thief, Cthulhu City, Dagger in the Heart and the upcoming Merryshire Detective Club; his novels include <em>The Gutter Prayer<\/em>, <em>The Lands of the Firstborn<\/em> trilogy and the upcoming <em>Dungeon Book<\/em>.<\/p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Find him at:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https:\/\/garhanrahan.com\/\">Author&rsquo;s Website<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a id=\"https:\/\/bsky.app\/profile\/mytholder.bsky.social\" href=\"https:\/\/bsky.app\/profile\/mytholder.bsky.social\" type=\"link\">Bluesky<\/a><\/li>\n<\/ul>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/ppd8174de1a4ecb90c3180566c78ec6c18.jpg"}},{"id":"R0005","name":["Ryan Cahill"],"sortname":"Cahill Ryan","prog":["65","77","111","134"],"bio":"<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Ryan Cahill is the Sunday Times and USA Today Bestselling author of the epic fantasy series, The Bound and The Broken.<\/p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Born and raised in Ireland, Ryan spent some years honing his craft amongst the rolling hills and hobbit holes of Middle-Earth, New Zealand, before returning home to conquer the world.<\/p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">He does not own enough swords, would sell his left kidney for a dragon egg, and despises mushrooms in all their eldritch forms.<\/p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">There are three things Ryan has always told himself about writing. Write the books you want to read. Write the books that your younger self would be proud of you for reading. Make sure they have dragons.<\/p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Find him at:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ryancahillauthor.com\/\">Author&rsquo;s Website<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"https:\/\/www.facebook.com\/login\/?next=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.facebook.com%2Fryancahillauthor%2F\">Facebook<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"https:\/\/www.instagram.com\/ryancahillauthor\/\">Instagram<\/a><\/li>\n<\/ul>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/pp0a5345866768b534def8795aace4a5ca.jpg"}},{"id":"R0006","name":["Ruth Ennis"],"sortname":"Ennis Ruth","prog":["143","144","147"],"bio":"<div dir=\"ltr\">Ruth Ennis (she\/they) is a writer from Kildare. Her work has been published in The Irish Writer&rsquo;s Handbook (Books Ireland) and I Am The Wind (Little Island). Ruth&rsquo;s debut novel Shorelines (Little Island) is a young adult verse novel retelling of The Little Mermaid, told through the lens of a fat protagonist. Ruth is usually working with books in some capacity. She was the Emerging Writer in Residence at the dlr LexIcon 2025-2026. She regularly reviews children&rsquo;s and young adult books on various platforms. Her main reviews can be found on the Books Ireland Magazine website. She is also a Book Doctor (Professional Book-Recommender) for Children's Books Ireland.<\/div>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/ppcb81301dbc1c6399d2fc707a43c7dd6e.png"}},{"id":"R0007","name":["John Coxon"],"sortname":"Coxon John","prog":["71","93","138","145"],"bio":"<p>John is a scientist by day, working on the interaction between the solar wind and Earth&rsquo;s magnetic field, and won the Royal Astronomical Society&rsquo;s Fowler Award. By night, he co-hosts the <strong>Octothorpe<\/strong> podcast. He is a keen tabletop gamer and GMs regularly; in all the other parts of the day, he alternates between feeling guilty he&rsquo;s not keeping up with his TV shows\/playing enough games\/reducing his to-read pile.<\/p>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/ppb69767ac1cb888e707855c0b9c2a1656.jpg"}},{"id":"R0008","name":["Liz Batty"],"sortname":"Batty Liz","prog":["71","138"],"bio":"<p>Liz is a fan, conrunner, and podcaster who started attending conventions in 2003 and has had strong opinions about them ever since. She is one-third of the <strong>Octothorpe<\/strong> podcast, launched in 2020 to cover fandom and interesting fannish topics. When she isn't podcasting she researches tropical diseases in Thailand.&nbsp;<\/p>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/ppf9cb8266c818f402dc10bab933f88540.png"}},{"id":"R0009","name":["Alison Scott"],"sortname":"Scott Alison","prog":["71","93","138"],"bio":"<p>Alison Scott is a London-based SF fan, and one third of the Hugo-winning podcast <strong>Octothorpe<\/strong>. She&rsquo;s also won awards in the past for her fan art, as well as the fanzine she co-edited, <em>Plokta<\/em>. She&rsquo;s worked on too many conventions to count and can often be found in the bar.<br \/><br \/>In 2026 she is playing a different board game every day of the year and will talk about this to anyone at length. She is looking forward to 2027.<\/p>\n<p id=\"docs-internal-guid-2d52ffcf-7fff-6505-63ce-4cb82f2ef6d3\" dir=\"ltr\">Find her at <a href=\"https:\/\/linktr.ee\/alisonscott\">https:\/\/linktr.ee\/alisonscott<\/a> and as bohemiancoast on most social media sites.<\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/pp024714a32f9cc3ecffc6687b398779ab.jpg"}},{"id":"R0010","name":["S.M. Hallow"],"sortname":"Hallow S.M.","prog":["135","144","149"],"bio":"<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">S. M. Hallow is a speculative fiction author who loves history, fairy tales, and Frankenstein. Her short fiction has received various award nominations and has appeared in the anthology <em>The Best of Fantasy, Volume 3<\/em>. Her longer fiction includes the novella <em>How to Survive This Fairytale<\/em>&mdash;a finalist in 7 categories for the 2025 Indie Ink Awards&mdash;and <em>The Halls of the Dead<\/em>, her debut novel with Harper Voyager.<\/p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Find her at:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Instagram &amp; Threads: @smhallowink<\/li>\n<li>Bluesky: <a href=\"https:\/\/bsky.app\/profile\/smhallow.bsky.social\">@smhallow.bsky.social<\/a><\/li>\n<li>Tumblr: <a href=\"http:\/\/smhallow.tumblr.com\/\">smhallow.tumblr.com<\/a><\/li>\n<li>Substack: <a href=\"http:\/\/smhallow.substack.com\/\">smhallow.substack.com<\/a><\/li>\n<li>Website: <a href=\"http:\/\/www.smhallow.com\/\">www.smhallow.com<\/a><\/li>\n<\/ul>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/pp2af51fec029d0396fc58768a6e9d907c.png"}},{"id":"R0011","name":["Jean B\u00fcrlesk"],"sortname":"B\u00fcrlesk Jean","prog":["104","136","144","146","149"],"bio":"<div id=\"comp-ifxxb4vh\" data-testid=\"richTextElement\">\n<p>Jean B&uuml;rlesk is a storyteller. He writes, he reads, he acts, he makes jokes nobody understands. He would sing and dance, but he has no sense of rhythm or melody. Sometimes he still sings and dances. As a Luxembourger and a lover of words, he expresses himself in five recognizable languages, as well as the usual nonsense.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<div id=\"comp-ifxxef89\" data-testid=\"richTextElement\">\n<p>He&rsquo;s a terrific guide, unless of course he&rsquo;s not and people just don&rsquo;t have the heart to tell him.<\/p>\n<p>\u200bHis debut short story collection 'The Pleasure of Drowning' won the Prix d&rsquo;Encouragement de la Fondation Servais 2019 and he was awarded a Chrysalis Award at Eurocon 2020.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/pp1e66b91d495d3fc7341d157da3001b73.jpg"}},{"id":"R0022","name":["Paul Carroll"],"sortname":"Carroll Paul","prog":["74","88","89","90","105","111","153"],"bio":"<p>Paul Carroll is a writer and comic creator from Dublin, with a special interest in horror, the supernatural, and mythology. He is a founding member of Limit Break Comics and Cup&aacute;n Fae, and author of The Black Pages.<\/p>\n<p>paulcarrollwriter.com<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"R0026","name":["Karina Steffens"],"sortname":"Steffens Karina","prog":["84","88","90","103","106"],"bio":"<p>Karina Steffens lives in Dublin and co-owns a boutique web design agency. Born in Ukraine, she emigrated twice and settled down in Ireland at the turn of the millennium. A lifelong bookwyrm, she devoured science fiction, fantasy, and Slavic folklore and amassed a small hoard of books, which is yet to achieve L-Space or open a portal to another world. To help it along, she turned her hand to writing. Her studies include BAs in Communication and Journalism and International Relations. Her stories and a poem have appeared in Penumbric Spec Fiction Mag, Empyreome, Gathering Storm, and the anthology Runs Like Clockwork from Wyldblood Press.<\/p>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/pp904f36355c32c3722debf18b0960d04a.jpg"}},{"id":"R0028","name":["April Steenburgh"],"sortname":"","prog":["92","93","144"],"bio":"<p>April Steenburgh is the pen name of Burdock Broughton. They live in Cornwall with a cunning little cat they found in a swamp. That cat might be a witch. Two other special rescue felines take up bed space and get their tails in the tea. April writes short fiction, primarily mythic fantasy, and started Faery Cat Press with friend Laura Anne Gilman. When not writing, April wanders the moors, walks the coast path, and provides academic support to college students as an English Professor and eSports Coach.<\/p>\n<p>You can find April through their <a href=\"https:\/\/www.aprilsteenburgh.com\">website<\/a>, <a href=\"https:\/\/www.patreon.com\/cw\/asteenburgh\">Patreon<\/a>, <a href=\"https:\/\/bsky.app\/profile\/burdockbroughton.bsky.social\">Bluesky<\/a>, and <a href=\"https:\/\/www.facebook.com\/burdockb\/\">Facebook<\/a>.<\/p>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/pp6373dccad586b6ffc289cfa7f2b52ffc.png"}},{"id":"R0029","name":["Alex McKenzie"],"sortname":"McKenzie Alex","prog":["90","102","110","167"],"bio":"<p>Just a reader.<\/p>\n<p>Wait, what am I saying? JUST a reader? If there weren't readers then authors and artists really wouldn't sell books, and there wouldn't be as many interesting discussions or ideas and inspirations taken from books and potentially made manifest in this world (don't ask me about other worlds, I haven't been there so can't say).&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p>I am proud to be a reader, and have ideas and inspirations and discussions and spread the word about good stories and have opinions on things.<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"R0036","name":["Finn McLellan"],"sortname":"McLellan Finn","prog":["92","143","149"],"bio":"<p class=\"has-small-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">Finn McLellan (they\/he) is an autistic disabled transmasc butch fantasy writer, hailing from the wilds of the northern UK.<\/p>\n<p class=\"has-small-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">When they&rsquo;re not writing they&rsquo;re busy immersing themselves in a variety of ridiculous hobbies, most of which also involve disappearing into fantasy worlds and\/or waving swords at people.<\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"R0040","name":["Antiqueight"],"sortname":"Antiqueight","prog":["67","75","111","155"],"bio":"<p>I'm an engineer, a science, fiction, fantasy, history and Ireland fan. I write Wikipedia articles to improve the balance of women's biographies especially where it relates to one of those topics. I have over 800 articles written so far. I have been coming to Cons since Catie twisted my arm back in 2007 and I travelled to Maynooth with no idea what a con was or who would be there. I was hooked by the realisation that these people were like me. I've been to NYCC, Worldcon and a Eurocon. Thanks to these cons I've gotten to meet and even become friends with some of my heroes.&nbsp;<\/p>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/pp268f0e7efd161c1ad57a42d58954ab39.png"}},{"id":"R0049","name":["Tommy Ferguson"],"sortname":"Ferguson Tommy","prog":["76","99"],"bio":"<p>Tommy Ferguson is a Belfast fan who got into fandom in the early 1980s via the now defunct Irish Science Fiction Association - and never looked back.<\/p>\n<p>He attended his first EasterCon a few years later - Albacon 3 in Glasgow - and had so much fun decided run his own convention in Belfast in 1986. Four further NICons followed, culminating in the National Universities&rsquo; convention in 1989. In the 1990s these morphed into a series of ten conventions called Mecons run by the Queen's University of Belfast Science Fiction and Fantasy society; which he founded in 1985.<\/p>\n<p>Since then, he has been actively involved in Belfast SF group, attending conventions in Ireland, the UK and all over the world, unsuccessfully running for TAFF, was a Corflu 50 representative and generally been present in fandom. A brief sojourn to Toronto in 1997 saw him found the First Thursday meeting, modelled after the London Tun meetings, when he discovered there was no social hub for Toronto fandom. The meeting is still going strong to this day.<\/p>\n<p>As fanzine fan he has published many issues of his perzine TommyWorld, a number of genzines and contributed to fanzines all over the world. As chair of Corflu Craic in 2023 he finally brought the fanzine fans' convention to his home city - and as Co-Chair of Reconnect - the 2025 EasterCon in Belfast - has realised a long-held ambition to close the circle from Albacon in 1986 - bringing Eastercon to the 'home' of British fandom.<\/p>\n<p>I&rsquo;ll be on the fan tables flogging memberships, taking part in panels or in the bar; say hello!<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"R0050","name":["RB Kelly"],"sortname":"Kelly Rachael","prog":["84","146","147"],"bio":"<p><span id=\"docs-internal-guid-b19788f5-7fff-266f-1fd9-f39d748143be\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-variant: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre-wrap;\">RB Kelly is the author of two novels: <\/span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-style: italic; font-variant: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre-wrap;\">Edge of Heaven<\/span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-variant: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre-wrap;\">, which was shortlisted for the Arthur C Clarke Award, and its sequel, <\/span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-style: italic; font-variant: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre-wrap;\">On The Brink<\/span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-variant: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre-wrap;\">. Her short stories have appeared in magazines and anthologies from around the world including The Best of British Science Fiction, Aurealis, Andromeda Spaceways Magazine, Lamplight, and ParSec, and her debut short story collection, <\/span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-style: italic; font-variant: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre-wrap;\">Different Wavelengths<\/span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-variant: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre-wrap;\">, came out earlier this year. She has a PhD in film theory and published her doctoral thesis as the non-fiction work <\/span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-style: italic; font-variant: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre-wrap;\">Mark Antony in Popular Culture<\/span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-variant: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre-wrap;\">. She lives just outside Belfast with her husband, two children, and a very lazy cat.<\/span><\/span><\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"R0052","name":["David Green"],"sortname":"Green David","prog":["77","105","163"],"bio":null,"links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/pp66ec3c517f2315f32f3c62cd32412503.png"}},{"id":"R0053","name":["Declan J"],"sortname":"Declan J","prog":["76","79","87"],"bio":"<p>Declan J is an Irish fan who has volunteered for Octocon, Worldcons and Eastercon. He is a Star Trek fan and enjoys reading space operas, cosy stories and listening to podcasts. He is disabled and is interested in assistive technology and hacking and making electronics.&nbsp;<\/p>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/pp"}},{"id":"R0054","name":["Brian Nisbet"],"sortname":"Nisbet Brian","prog":["110"],"bio":"<p><span style=\"color: #15181b; font-family: 'Open Sans', Lato, 'Helvetica Neue', Calibri, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 20px; background-color: #fdfdfd;\">Brian Nisbet started running running gaming (Leprecon, Gaelcon, K2) and SF conventions in Ireland in the mid-90s. In 1998 he got involved in the International Discworld Convention, held in the UK, and went on to chair it twice in 2010 &amp; 2012. He co-chaired Shamrokon, the 2014 Eurocon. Since then he's mostly been on the committees of Worldcons (Helsinki, San Jose, Dublin, Washington DC, Chicago, Glasgow) or Worldcon bids (Dublin in 2029) in a variety of roles, including substantial work (along with many others) to implement and improve codes of conduct. He has written for a number of published TTRPGs as well as many, many convention scenarios. He's been a huge fan of fantasy, sci-fi and gaming for over 30 years and he's so looking forward to all the things to come!<\/span><\/p>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/ppb54dbdd61340903f37815c73a345ef28.png"}},{"id":"R0062","name":["David Bamford"],"sortname":"Bamford David","prog":["105"],"bio":"<p>David has contributed to the Bram Stoker Award-nominated essay collection Darkest Margins, about liminal states and spaces in the horror genre. His primary genre interests are SFF novels, horror films and novels, and his current work in progress places particular focus on the role of grief and queerness in the slasher movie. He lives in Glasgow with his husband Alan Fleming, and together they are co-chairing Unconfined, the Eastercon in Glasgow in 2027.<\/p>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/ppdf0612ae71ba12c4d27d2c64888ae176.png"}},{"id":"R0063","name":["Deirdre  Thornton"],"sortname":"Thornton  Deirdre","prog":["76","79","88"],"bio":null,"links":[]},{"id":"R0066","name":["Colm Dodd"],"sortname":"Dodd Colm","prog":["77"],"bio":null,"links":[]},{"id":"R0070","name":["Clare O'Beara"],"sortname":"O'Beara Clare","prog":["90"],"bio":"<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14.0pt; font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif; color: #181818; background: white;\">Clare O'Beara is a tree surgeon and expert witness, and a former national standard showjumper. She has qualified in multimedia journalism, data visualisation, media law, ESG law, artificial intelligence, rewilding, and ecology, and writes on environmental themes. She has independently published 19 books, including four science fiction and several YA books. <\/span><\/p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14.0pt; font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif; color: #181818; background: white;\">Clare is an award&ndash;winning writer, award-winning blogger, and award-winning photojournalist, whose journalism work has been published in more than thirty countries. Her credits include&nbsp;<em>Writing.ie, The Register.com, Mensa Magazine<\/em>&nbsp;and Mensa&nbsp;<em>International Journal.<\/em>&nbsp;Editor of&nbsp;<em>Inside DBS,<\/em>&nbsp;the official blog of Dublin Business School. Photo credits include the <em>Daily Mail, Classic Hits Radio,<\/em> <em>Radio Nova, The MJ,<\/em> and <em>Extra.ie<\/em>. <\/span><\/p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14.0pt; font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif; color: #181818; background: white;\">Clare reviews books for Fresh Fiction, a media website in Texas, USA. <\/span><\/p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14.0pt; font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif; color: #181818; background: white;\">Top 1% Goodreads Reviewer.<\/span><\/p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.goodreads.com\/author\/show\/7244400.Clare_O_Beara\"><span style=\"font-size: 14.0pt; font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;\">https:\/\/www.goodreads.com\/author\/show\/7244400.Clare_O_Beara<\/span><\/a><\/p>\n<p><a href=\"https:\/\/www.linkedin.com\/in\/clare-o-beara-0564a26b\/\"><span style=\"font-size: 14.0pt; font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;\">https:\/\/www.linkedin.com\/in\/clare-o-beara-0564a26b\/<\/span><\/a><\/p>\n<p><a href=\"https:\/\/www.clareobeara.ie\/\"><span style=\"font-size: 14.0pt; font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif; background: white;\">https:\/\/www.clareobeara.ie\/<\/span><\/a><\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"R0073","name":["Val Nolan"],"sortname":"Nolan Val","prog":["99"],"bio":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Val Nolan is co-author of the BSFA\/BFA shortlisted and Locus recommended writing guide series <em>Spec for Newbies: A Beginner&rsquo;s Guide to Writing the Subgenres of Science Fiction, Fantasy, and Horror<\/em> (Luna Press, 2023-2026) and is author of the monograph <em>Neil Jordan: Works for the Page<\/em> (Cork University Press, 2022). He has published peer-reviewed articles in <em>Science Fiction Studies<\/em>, <em>Journal of Graphic Novels and Comics<\/em>, <em>Irish University Review<\/em>, <em>Foundation<\/em>, and <em>Symplok\u0113<\/em>. He has published chapters in many academic books on topics including <em>Star Trek<\/em>, <em>Fringe<\/em>, <em>Lost<\/em>, <em>Battlestar Galactica<\/em>, and <em>Stargate<\/em>. He has also published short stories in <em>Year's Best Science Fiction<\/em>, <em>Best of British Science Fiction<\/em>, and elsewhere. He writes a regular column in <em>Interzone <\/em>looking at the history of SFF criticism, contributes to <em>Reactor<\/em>, and reviews books for the <em>Irish Times<\/em>. His current project is a history of space opera&rsquo;s first hundred years (forthcoming 2028). Find him on Bluesky: <a title=\"Val Nolan on BlueSky\" href=\"https:\/\/bsky.app\/profile\/vallescurarule.bsky.social\">@vallescurarule.bsky.social<\/a>.<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"R0077","name":["Jack-John Kirwan"],"sortname":"Kirwan Jack-John","prog":["76","102","106"],"bio":"<p>Ireland's foremost and only Pok&eacute;mon Professor (until challenged on that point), Jack-John Kirwan has been an academic, playwright, and archivist.<\/p>\n<p><br \/>Jack-John Kirwan has a PHD from studying the creative process by looking at <em>Twitch Plays Pok&eacute;mon<\/em> in the academically titled&nbsp;<em>'An investigation into how distributed creative communities engage in the creative process through a quantitative and qualitative analysis of Twitch Plays Pok&eacute;mon&rsquo;s narratives'. <\/em>Which is available on Maynooth's website, or a shorter version is available via '<em>The Routledge Handbook of Fan Video and Digital Authorship'<\/em> for the price of 'Dear god do not buy it'.<br \/><br \/>With Trench Entertainment Jack-John Kirwan wrote historical black comedies about the Soviet Troika's (<em>Troika<\/em>) and Operation Paperclip (<em>Du<span class=\"font-semibold\" data-streamdown=\"strong\">&AElig;<\/span>l<\/em>) as well as adaptions of Edgar Allen Poe (<em>Telltale William<\/em>) and satire about Ireland's theatre scene (<em>Playing at Plays<\/em>).<\/p>\n<p><br \/><em>The Gaia Starship<\/em> will debut in 2028 as a sci-fi coming-of-age story.<\/p>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/ppa3c664ff86793af10509c107823eea73.png"}},{"id":"R0086","name":["Gillian Polack"],"sortname":"Polack Gillian","prog":["87","104","150"],"bio":"<p style=\"line-height: 135%; margin-top: 0.42cm; margin-bottom: 0cm;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><strong>Dr Gillian POLACK<\/strong><\/span><span style=\"color: #000000;\"> is a Jewish Australian speculative fiction writer <\/span><span style=\"color: #000000;\">with too many doctorates<\/span><span style=\"color: #000000;\">. Awards include the 2020 Ditmar award and the A. Bertram Chandler (lifetime achievement in science fiction). Her novel <\/span><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><em>The Year of the Fruitccake<\/em><\/span><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><span style=\"font-style: normal;\"> won a Ditmar <\/span><\/span><span style=\"color: #000000;\">award and was shortlisted for the Aurealis awards<\/span><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><em>. <\/em><\/span><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Her short story in <\/span><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><em>Other Covenants<\/em><\/span><span style=\"color: #000000;\"> (ed. Lobel and Shainblum)<\/span><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><em> <\/em><\/span><span style=\"color: #000000;\">was a finalist for the Sidewise Award<\/span><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><em>. <\/em><\/span><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><span style=\"font-style: normal;\">Her next novel, <\/span><\/span><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><em>After Empire<\/em><\/span><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><em> <\/em><\/span><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><span style=\"font-style: normal;\">will be released <\/span><\/span><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><span style=\"font-style: normal;\">in the US <\/span><\/span><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><span style=\"font-style: normal;\">in 202<\/span><\/span><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><span style=\"font-style: normal;\">7<\/span><\/span><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><span style=\"font-style: normal;\">.<\/span><\/span><\/p>\n<p style=\"line-height: 135%; margin-top: 0.42cm; margin-bottom: 0cm;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><span style=\"font-style: normal;\">When not writing fiction, s<\/span><\/span><span style=\"color: #000000;\">he is an ethnohistorian <\/span><span style=\"color: #000000;\">and <\/span><span style=\"color: #000000;\">a Research Fellow at Deakin University with a special interest in how story transmits culture. Her most recent scholarly book is <\/span><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><em>Story Matrices: Cultural Encoding and Cultural Baggage in the Worlds of Science Fiction and Fantasy<\/em><\/span><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><em>. <\/em><\/span><\/p>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/ppab2310c0d72513c992ed52a69535488b.png"}},{"id":"R0087","name":["Ruth Frances Long \/Jessica Thorne"],"sortname":"Long Ruth","prog":["74","79","95","114"],"bio":"<p style=\"box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0px 0px 24px; color: #141412; font-family: 'Source Sans Pro', Helvetica, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; background-color: #ffffff;\">Ruth Frances Long \/ Jessica Thorne writes romance and fantasy from the heart which often strays into weird and wonderful liminal places. She works in a specialized library of rare &amp; occasionally crazy books.<\/p>\n<p style=\"box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0px 0px 24px; color: #141412; font-family: 'Source Sans Pro', Helvetica, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; background-color: #ffffff;\">As Ruth Frances Long she writes fantasy, often about scary fairies. In 2015 she won the European Science Fiction Society Spirit of Dedication Award for Best Author of Children&rsquo;s Science Fiction and Fantasy for <em style=\"box-sizing: border-box;\">A Crack in Everything<\/em>. Her latest series <em style=\"box-sizing: border-box;\">Feral Gods<\/em>, an adult fantasy trilogy from Hodderscape featuring &ldquo;an alternate-Renaissance fantasy world and inspired by the early days of printing in Europe&rdquo; begins with <em style=\"box-sizing: border-box;\">The Book of Gold<\/em>, continues with <em style=\"box-sizing: border-box;\">The Lore of Silver (November 2025) <\/em>and finishes with <em style=\"box-sizing: border-box;\">The God of Bronze (November 2026). <\/em><em style=\"box-sizing: border-box;\">The Book of Gold <\/em><span style=\"box-sizing: border-box;\">was longlisted for the 2025 BSFA award and was selected for the Locus Recommended Reading List for 2024. Ruth was Octocon's Guest of Honour in 2024<\/span><\/p>\n<p style=\"box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0px 0px 24px; color: #141412; font-family: 'Source Sans Pro', Helvetica, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; background-color: #ffffff;\">As Jessica Thorne she writes adult fantasy women&rsquo;s fiction, which wanders from Space Opera to time travel to epic fantasy, including <em style=\"box-sizing: border-box;\">The Lost Queen trilogy (A Touch of Shadows, A Kiss of Flame <\/em>and<em style=\"box-sizing: border-box;\"> A Crown of Darkness), The Water Witch, The Bookbinder&rsquo;s Daughter, The Lost Girl&rsquo;s of Foxfield Hall, The Queen&rsquo;s Wing, The Hollow King duology (Mageborn <\/em>and <em style=\"box-sizing: border-box;\">Nightborn). The Stone&rsquo;s Heart<\/em> was nominated for the Romantic Novelists&rsquo; Association Romantic Fantasy novel of the year in 2020. Her Irish gothic romance <em style=\"box-sizing: border-box;\">Wildewood <\/em>came out in January 2026. Her new cosy fantasy series with Boldwood began in September 2026 with <em>The Teacup Dragon Sanctuary<\/em>, followed in Spring 2027 by <em>The Magical Emporium by the Sea.<\/em><\/p>\n<p style=\"box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0px 0px 24px; color: #141412; font-family: 'Source Sans Pro', Helvetica, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; background-color: #ffffff;\">&nbsp;<\/p>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/ppd2809d2ce2429d8c55e0bb79e03b804b.png"}},{"id":"R0088","name":["Michael Carroll"],"sortname":"Carroll Michael","prog":["77","89","95","107"],"bio":"<p>Writer of novels and comics, caretaker of the still-not-outlawed Rusty Staples Comics Blog, keen amateur penguin juggler (non-practicing), recipient of the Most Effective Longevity Potion Award 1272 A.D., former World's Youngest Person, one of only two people to have attended every single Octocon.<\/p>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/ppad537621bcacbca1a8cf9fd13ff35e19.jpg"}},{"id":"R0089","name":["Phaze D'Out"],"sortname":"Phaze D'Out","prog":["81","90","110","172","173"],"bio":"<p>First entry to sci fi was seeing a new hope on release in the Ambassador cinema in Dublin. Dove happily into consuming genre tv and fiction in every available format.<\/p>\n<p>Attended first con, for discworld in 2006 and have been an enthusiastic atendee of numerous cons ever since. Strong interest in the science behind sci fi but just love everything which speculated about worlds and universes beyond our own<\/p>\n<p>Reads constantly, gets paranoid if words aren't close to hand<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"R0097","name":["Philippa Ryder"],"sortname":"Ryder Philippa","prog":["113"],"bio":"<p>Part of the original Irish Science Fiction Association from 1976 onwards, including periods as Chair, Secretary etc. Original committee member of ISFACon 1 and 2, Octocon 1990-1992, TImewarp 1 and 2, etc. Part of the editorial team that published Albedo One magazine and Aeon Press books. Long-term fan, interested in hard sf, fandom, sf history. Currently writing a queer science fiction novel, may never get it published but then I thought that too about my memoir which was published by Mercier Press a few years ago, https:\/\/www.mercierpress.ie\/books\/my-name-is-philippa\/&nbsp; &nbsp;Also working intermittently on an Irish SF fan history with my wife Helen, it's a big job!<\/p>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/pp4ceb24997c84c229fa969622876d251b.png"}},{"id":"R0101","name":["Fionnuala Murphy"],"sortname":"Murphy Fionnuala","prog":["68"],"bio":null,"links":[]},{"id":"R0102","name":["Emma Newman"],"sortname":"Newman Emma","prog":["70","83","102","114","119","137"],"bio":"<p>Emma Newman writes short stories, novels and novellas in multiple speculative fiction genres. Her novel, After Atlas, was shortlisted for the Arthur C. Clarke award and the third and fourth novels in the Planetfall series have been shortlisted for a BSFA Best Novel award. The series was shortlisted for the Best Series Hugo Award. Emma is also a professional audiobook narrator, and won the Alfie and Hugo Best Fancast Awards for her podcast Tea and Jeopardy. She is the co-host of the podcast Starship Alexandria. For more information go to <a href=\"https:\/\/www.enewman.co.uk\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">www.enewman.co.uk<\/a><\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"R0103","name":["E\u00f3in Dooley"],"sortname":"Dooley E\u00f3in","prog":["81","88","113"],"bio":"<p><span style=\"box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0px; padding: 0px; border: 0px; font-variant-numeric: inherit; font-variant-east-asian: inherit; font-variant-alternates: inherit; font-variant-position: inherit; font-variant-emoji: inherit; font-weight: bolder; font-stretch: inherit; font-size: 21.3333px; line-height: inherit; font-family: 'Source Sans Pro', sans-serif; font-optical-sizing: inherit; font-size-adjust: inherit; font-kerning: inherit; font-feature-settings: inherit; font-variation-settings: inherit; font-language-override: inherit; vertical-align: baseline; color: #575757; -webkit-text-fill-color: currentcolor; text-align: justify; background-color: #ffffff;\">E&oacute;in Dooley<\/span><span style=\"color: #575757; font-family: 'Source Sans Pro', sans-serif; font-size: 21.3333px; text-align: justify; background-color: #ffffff;\"> (he\/him) is a writer from central Ireland, and a member of SFWA and BFS. Having completed a master's degree in cognitive science and philosophy, he turned to creative fiction, primarily to stave off a PhD. This appears to be working. His debut book was No Sympathy, an urban fantasy novella published by Android Press.&nbsp;<\/span><\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"R0114","name":["Mark Mullan"],"sortname":"Mullan Mark","prog":["145"],"bio":null,"links":[]},{"id":"R0117","name":["Sarah Johnson"],"sortname":"Johnson Sarah","prog":["141"],"bio":"<p>Sarah Johnson is an Australian born Neurodivergent feminist, historian and theatre maker based in Brighton, England. A precocious reader from the very beginning, Sarah was introduced very early on to stories, folk tales and Greek mythology by her Mum and never looked back. This provided her with a deep fascination for tales from all over the world from the fictional to the historic and everything in between. Sarah has spent the last two years researching the origin and development of the Medusa myth leading to a one hour talk on her recent emergence as an protective icon for SA Survivors and most recently the Me Too movement. Sarah is currently writing a play based on this research with Medusa and the modern epidemic of extreme violence against women at it's heart, investigating how we can collectively work to \"change the story\" and end a cycle that harms us all.<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"R0121","name":["Fergus Deffely"],"sortname":"Deffely Fergus","prog":["83","89"],"bio":"<p>Fergus Deffely grew up in county Mayo, in Ireland, and was introduced to the arts in Galway city. He spent almost fifteen years working as a software developer, where he learned certain principles of design and construction which have found a new life in the dreaming and building of poems and stories.<\/p>\n<p>He is the writer of two novels: <em>The Fix<\/em> is a rural noir that takes place in the West of Ireland. <em>The Disturbance<\/em> is a genre eclectic tale of cats and alternate worlds. His short fiction has been published in Spellweaver and in The Corner Bar magazines.<\/p>\n<p>You can find some of his poems and stories at: <a href=\"https:\/\/preciousflaws.wordpress.com\">preciousflaws.wordpress.com<\/a><\/p>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/ppb6b43bc63b0216c4cba279c56cb2693e.png"}},{"id":"R0126","name":["Anatoly Belilovsky"],"sortname":"Belilovsky Anatoly","prog":["83","104","111"],"bio":"<p><span style=\"color: #222222; font-family: arial, sans-serif; font-size: small; background-color: #ffffff;\">Anatoly Belilovsky was born in<\/span><span class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"color: #222222; font-family: arial, sans-serif; font-size: small; background-color: #ffffff;\"> <\/span><span style=\"color: #222222; font-family: arial, sans-serif; font-size: small; background-color: #ffffff;\">a city that has changed owners six or seven times in the last century. He was traded to the US for a truckload of wheat and a defector to be named later, learned English from Star Trek reruns, and went on to become a SFWA member in spite of a chronic cat deficiency. In his spare time, he practices pediatrics in New York<\/span><span class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"color: #222222; font-family: arial, sans-serif; font-size: small; background-color: #ffffff;\">.<\/span><\/p>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/pp6caca0cea12603e615b2a2903abde7e6.png"}},{"id":"R0134","name":["Edmund Schluessel"],"sortname":"Schluessel Edmund","prog":["102","145"],"bio":"<p><strong>Dr. Edmund Schluessel <\/strong>(they\/them) is an astrophysicist, teacher, science communicator and science fiction writer based in Helsinki, Finland. They have presented on such diverse topics as the practicalities of turning the sun into a rocket ship, the use of pedagogical techniques to make better convention program items, and how&nbsp;<em>Star Trek<\/em> episodes are mythologically related to Shakespeare's&nbsp;\"The Tempest.\" In their spare time, they engage in socialist activism and appreciate the existence of cats. You can find them on BlueSky @spacecurves.bsky.social and on Facebook at http:\/\/facebook.com\/spacecurves.<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"R0140","name":["Juliet E McKenna"],"sortname":"McKenna  Juliet E","prog":["84","141","144"],"bio":"<p class=\"western\" style=\"margin-bottom: 0cm; line-height: 100%;\" align=\"left\">Juliet E McKenna lives in the Cotswolds, UK. Her epic fantasy debut, <em>The Thief&rsquo;s Gamble<\/em>, began <em>The Tales of Einarinn<\/em> in 1999, followed by <em>The Aldabreshin Compass<\/em> sequence, <em>The Chronicles of the Lescari Revolution<\/em>, and <em>The Hadrumal Crisis<\/em> trilogies. <em>The Riven Kingdom, <\/em><span style=\"font-style: normal;\">set in <\/span><span style=\"font-style: normal;\">a very different fantasy worl<\/span><span style=\"font-style: normal;\">d, is published 27<\/span><sup><span style=\"font-style: normal;\">th<\/span><\/sup><span style=\"font-style: normal;\"> October<\/span><em> <\/em><span style=\"font-style: normal;\">2026.<\/span><\/p>\n<p class=\"western\" style=\"margin-bottom: 0cm; line-height: 100%;\" align=\"left\"><em>The Green Man&rsquo;s Heir<\/em> began her contemporary fantasy series inspired by myth and folklore in 2018. <em>The Green Man&rsquo;s Quarry <\/em><span style=\"font-style: normal;\">won the BSFA Award for Best Novel 2023, and <\/span><em>The Green Man&rsquo;s Debt<\/em><span style=\"font-style: normal;\"> is the<\/span><span style=\"font-style: normal;\"> most recent <\/span><span style=\"font-style: normal;\">title <\/span><span style=\"font-style: normal;\">in September 2026<\/span><span style=\"font-style: normal;\">. Her<\/span> shorter fiction includes forays into dark fantasy, steampunk, science fiction and shared world projects, while her <span style=\"font-style: normal;\">novel <\/span><em>The Cleaving<\/em> is a female-centred retelling of King Arthur&rsquo;s story.<\/p>\n<p class=\"western\" style=\"margin-bottom: 0cm; line-height: 100%;\" align=\"left\">She has served as a judge for the James White Award, the Aeon Award, the Arthur C Clarke Award and the World Fantasy Awards. In 2015 she received the British Fantasy Society&rsquo;s Karl Edward Wagner Award.<\/p>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/pp3608484e34b1c8bca2fcdc3331454091.png"}},{"id":"R0141","name":["Farah Mendlesohn"],"sortname":"Mendlesohn Farah","prog":["84","147"],"bio":"<p>Farah Mendlesohn is a conrunner and a writer of non-fiction. This year they were the GUFF delegate to Swancon.<\/p>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/pp416d4a56135ea3f6fff2489c23544544.png"}},{"id":"R0149","name":["Jonny Nexus"],"sortname":"Nexus Jonny","prog":["102"],"bio":"<p>Jonny Nexus has been writing SF and fantasy in various forms since creating a cult RPG humour webzine, <em>Critical Miss<\/em>, in the late '90s. Having since self-published four novels (<em>Game Night<\/em>, his tale of dysfunctional role-playing gods, <em>If Pigs Could Fly<\/em> and <em>Sticks and Stones<\/em> of the <em>West Kensington Paranormal Detective Agency Series<\/em>, and <em>The Sleeping Dragon<\/em>), he now has an agent, John Jarrold, but not yet a publishing contract. He&rsquo;s been attending various SF conventions since the 2008 Eastercon, Odyssey. (You might well have bought a book from him.) He&rsquo;s long stated that his ambition in writing is neither fame nor fortune, but simply being able to describe himself as a writer without feeling the need to deploy either quotes or a footnote.<\/p>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/pp70832110f134fe68beceadfc7c6f3d18.png"}},{"id":"R0151","name":["Kalin Nenov (Kal)"],"sortname":"Nenov Kalin","prog":["92","104","141","143","156"],"bio":"<p><span style=\"color: #222222; font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif; font-size: small; background-color: #ffffff;\">I'm a translator, editor, publisher, agent, promoter, and when there's no other choice, writer. Currently, I live in Sofia, Bulgaria. My translations have appeared in various magazines and anthologies, most notably <em>Asimov's Science Fiction<\/em> in 2024. I've been bipolar since 2003 and non-binary (more specifically, postgender) ever since I can remember myself. For twenty years, I've been part of the Human Library: a volunteer community promoting fiction that asks what it means to be human--and how we can expand the definition. You can find more here: <\/span><a style=\"color: #1155cc; font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif; font-size: small; background-color: #ffffff;\" href=\"https:\/\/choveshkata.net\/blog\/?page_id=36#English\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https:\/\/www.google.com\/url?q=https:\/\/choveshkata.net\/blog\/?page_id%3D36%23English&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1787643043493000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3c2_T0lgG9WENi4IQezo9B\">https:\/\/choveshkata.net\/blog\/?<wbr \/>page_id=36#English<\/a><\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"R0153","name":["GT Korbin"],"sortname":"Korbin GT","prog":["77","83","154"],"bio":null,"links":[]},{"id":"R0155","name":["Fi"],"sortname":"Fi","prog":["106","174"],"bio":null,"links":[]},{"id":"R0170","name":["Mona Tewari"],"sortname":"Tewari Mona","prog":["143","146","147"],"bio":"<p class=\"cvGsUA block-font-kerning-none block-font-feature-liga-off block-font-feature-clig-off block-font-feature-calt-off direction-ltr align-start\" style=\"--8sd0kg: none; --cyv3zq: 24px; --gjplxq: 1.4; --s8nbea: p; line-height: 33px;\"><span style=\"color: #4d472e; font-family: YAFcfnxhTZs_0, _fb_, auto;\"><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.28px; caret-color: #4d472e;\">Mona Tewari lives with her husband, daughters, and dogs in the northeastern part of the USA. After graduating from the California Institute of Technology (Caltech) with degrees in biology and philosophy, she completed dental school, residency, and a master's degree at the University of San Francisco, California. She joined a fantastic pediatric dental practice where she thought she'd be for a long time, but life had other plans.<\/span><\/span><\/p>\n<p class=\"cvGsUA block-font-kerning-none block-font-feature-liga-off block-font-feature-clig-off block-font-feature-calt-off direction-ltr align-start\" style=\"--8sd0kg: none; --cyv3zq: 24px; --gjplxq: 1.4; --s8nbea: p; line-height: 33px;\"><span style=\"color: #4d472e; font-family: YAFcfnxhTZs_0, _fb_, auto;\"><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.28px; caret-color: #4d472e;\">When health issues forced her to stop practicing, Mona turned to writing fiction. She has long been fascinated by the way that narratives of legends and histories create the lens through which we see our legends and histories and how these views shape our present. Plus, as the daughter of two immigrants from India, Mona didn't see herself much in popular media while growing up, and she is determined to show her daughters that they belong in every world.<\/span><\/span><\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"R0171","name":["Jonathan L. Howard"],"sortname":"Howard Jonathan L.","prog":["69"],"bio":null,"links":[]},{"id":"R0172","name":["Keren Landsman"],"sortname":"Landsman Keren","prog":["104","141","145"],"bio":null,"links":[]},{"id":"R0191","name":["Meg MacDonald"],"sortname":"MacDonald Meg","prog":["93"],"bio":null,"links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/ppe67a66fecc645c930dd0904be954dd7e.png"}},{"id":"Z0031","name":["MaryBrigid Turner"],"sortname":"Turner MaryBrigid","prog":["105","159"],"bio":"<p id=\"docs-internal-guid-fc8b1f61-7fff-006c-5474-40b6e007c502\" dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.38; margin-top: 0.0pt; margin-bottom: 0.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-variant: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre-wrap;\">Originally from Auckland, NZ, MaryBrigid Turner came to Ireland on a working holiday,&nbsp; walked through the doors of Hodges Figgis The Bookstore and never left. More than 20 years later, she helps run the Children's department there, looks after their Science Fiction and Fantasy monthly bookclub, and is actively involved with the wider book community in Ireland. MaryBrigid is also part of Octocon's organising committee, and programme team. <\/span><\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"Z0067","name":["Kat Dodd"],"sortname":"Dodd Kat","prog":["66","80","89","105","107","121","143","167"],"bio":"<p>Kat Dodd is an American transplant that escaped from a small town in Arkansas right between Toad Suck, Pickles Gap, and Wooster. The first story they ever wrote was a Star Trek fanfic writtten in a purple crayon in their journal, and Kat hasn&rsquo;t stopped writing since. Their typical genres are a conglomeration of fantasy\/sci-fi, LGBTQI+, and YA, with a thread of romance going through them. Kat lives with their Spouse-Type-Creature, though Kat frequently forgets to feed him, as well as an agent of chaos disguised as an assistance pup in training that Kat <em>does not<\/em> forget to feed.<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"Z0068","name":["S.L. Dove Cooper"],"sortname":"Cooper, S.L. Dove","prog":["87","141"],"bio":"<p><span style=\"color: #4d4d4d; font-family: proxima-nova, sans-serif; font-size: 17.6px; background-color: #ffffff;\">S.L. Dove Cooper (she\/they) is a queer author and editor. She spends her time exploring asexuality and aromanticism in literature, finally having found a good use for her MA. She has been chasing stories in some way or another since she was old enough to follow a narrative. Visit www.dovelynnwriter.com<\/span><\/p>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/ppf8bf57c931a0c33e9b2c4f64f7a2971c.jpg"}},{"id":"Z0075","name":["Siobh\u00e1n Murphy"],"sortname":"Murphy Siobhan","prog":["81","113","152"],"bio":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Times New Roman',serif;\">Siobh&aacute;n F. Murphy is a Belfast-based freelance writer and editor with a specialism in fiction writing and in ELT and educational publishing. <em>Hedge Witch<\/em>, the first book in <em>The Hedgefall Chronicles<\/em> was published in 2025 and&nbsp;<em>Mage Call<\/em>, the second in the trilogy was published in May 2026 by Uplit Press.<\/span><\/p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Times New Roman',serif;\">Siobh&aacute;n primarily writes fantasy and dystopian fiction, educational video scripts and has also written a comedy short story for BBC Northern Ireland, and a body horror story for <em>The Selkie<\/em>. She has a degree in English Literature and Spanish, and is currently studying part time for a Creative Writing MA. She lives with one bad-tempered cat called Ianto. In her free time, you can find her playing Dungeons and Dragons, getting involved in amateur theatre, making folded book artwork, trying to learn new languages, and, of course relaxing with a good novel (usually about the end of the world, whatever that says about her).<\/span><\/p>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/ppb31c5c623f2abbd7047b1b1e7f369ae0.png"}},{"id":"Z0077","name":["Naomi Kelly"],"sortname":"Kelly Naomi","prog":["79","95"],"bio":"<p>Naomi Kelly is an indie author from Cork, Ireland.<\/p>\n<p>Her most recent novel, <em>STARVED,<\/em> is a historical fantasy-meets-paranormal romance, inspired by the colonisation and Famine of Ireland.&nbsp;This haunting standalone novel follows one young woman's fight to live... even if it means becoming undead.&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p>Mythology, merfolk, and magic? What's not to love! <em>MERAKI: A SYREN STORY&nbsp;<\/em>is Kelly's my popular novel. This syren <em>'tail'&nbsp;<\/em>follows a runaway syren who finds herself trapped within the net of an enemy King who wishes to use her powerful voice as a weapon. \"Syrens are bound to tell the truth, but that doesn&rsquo;t mean their lives cannot be riddled with lies...\"&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p>And the SYREN STORY series contiues with sequel, KAIROS, and companion novel, KALON.&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><a href=\"https:\/\/www.instagram.com\/naomikellywriting\/\">Instagram<\/a><\/p>\n<p><a href=\"https:\/\/www.amazon.co.uk\/Starved-Naomi-Kelly\/dp\/B0FR28CVTX\/ref=tmm_hrd_swatch_0?_encoding=UTF8&amp;dib_tag=se&amp;dib=eyJ2IjoiMSJ9.-l2fetnEqKW2LM9UxmtRWQ.h8vBr2huJVRxxwc7_u_b2wXCeyiekQMHaGehzTONmJs&amp;qid=1788380135&amp;sr=8-1\">STARVED Hardback<\/a><\/p>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/ppa922d9c21eeb23459ad225d60b806dee.png"}},{"id":"Z0078","name":["Sofiia Sarantseva"],"sortname":null,"prog":["158"],"bio":"<p>A short-film producer for an emerging high-fantasy universe, Soul Atlas. I am representing Soul Atlas at Octocon and would be happy to share information about our comic, film, music and game production.<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"Z0080","name":["Bear Wiseman"],"sortname":"Wiseman Bear","prog":["87","93","146"],"bio":"<p>Bear Wiseman is a 40-year-old lifelong writer and fantasy lover, born in Canada but residing in Finland. Currently, she is publishing a long-form slice-of-life fantasy series called The Vitmar Chronicles, with its third installment set for release, along with a series reboot, in spring of 2027. Apart from writing fantasy, Bear also loves listening to music, herbalism, and getting unhinged in her garden.&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p>You can also find Bear published in the first two issues of Australia's Fabled Magazine.<\/p>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/ppc10f99ec06ffb0e47898dba90084e56c.png"}},{"id":"Z0081","name":["Vanessa MacLaren-Wray"],"sortname":"MacLaren-Wray Vanessa","prog":["149","151"],"bio":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Vanessa MacLaren-Wray (she\/her) creates worlds where people&mdash;human or otherwise&mdash;matter. She&rsquo;s the author of <em><span style=\"font-family: 'Calibri',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-bidi;\">All That Was Asked<\/span><\/em>, <em><span style=\"font-family: 'Calibri',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-bidi;\">Shadows of Insurrection,<\/span><\/em> and <em><span style=\"font-family: 'Calibri',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-bidi;\">Flames of Attrition<\/span><\/em>, plus the middle-grade space-opera novel <em>The Smugglers<\/em>. Her short fiction and poetry have appeared with Dragon Gems and in the award-winning <em><span style=\"font-family: 'Calibri',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-bidi;\">Fault Zone<\/span><\/em> anthologies. She guest-hosts for the podcast <em><span style=\"font-family: 'Calibri',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-bidi;\">Small Publishing in a Big Universe <\/span><\/em><em><span style=\"font-family: 'Calibri',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-bidi; font-style: normal;\">and is an active member of SFWA, Broad Universe, and Strong Women-Strange Worlds. When not arguing with her cats, she edits fellow writers&rsquo; fiction and poetry, revises her own works-in-progress, and claims to be managing her website, <\/span><\/em><em><span style=\"font-family: 'Calibri',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-bidi;\">cometarytales.com.<\/span><\/em><\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"Z0082","name":["Paul Anthony Shortt"],"sortname":"Shortt Paul Anthony","prog":["84","92","124"],"bio":"<p>Fantasy author and video essayist. Paul <span style=\"background-color: #ffffff; color: #373e3e; font-family: 'Amazon Ember', Ember, amazon_ember, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif; font-size: 15px; white-space: pre-wrap;\">believes in magic and monsters. In ghosts and fairies. The creatures that lurk under the bed and inside the closet. The things that live in the dark, and the heroes who stand against them. Above all, he believes that stories have the power to change the world, and the most important stories are the ones which show that monsters can be beaten.<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Author of the Lady Raven series and the Memory Wars Trilogy. Host of the Wordmaker's Workshop YouTube channel.<\/p>\n<p><a href=\"http:\/\/www.paulanthonyshortt.com\/\">http:\/\/www.paulanthonyshortt.com\/<\/a><\/p>\n<p><a href=\"https:\/\/www.youtube.com\/@wordmakersworkshop\">https:\/\/www.youtube.com\/@wordmakersworkshop<\/a>&nbsp;<\/p>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/pp34ac482872dcd7d2cbf96672f3262803.jpg"}},{"id":"Z0084","name":["Liz Bourke"],"sortname":"Bourke Liz","prog":["74","81","99","107"],"bio":"<p>Liz Bourke has a PhD in Classics and has written book reviews for Reactor, Locus, and Strange Horizons, among other places.&nbsp;<\/p>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/pp7e288936b17abe6e811f1014eb2c1427.png"}},{"id":"Z0085","name":["M.E.Moirin"],"sortname":"M.E.Moirin","prog":["74","107"],"bio":"<p><span style=\"caret-color: #ffffff; font-family: 'DM Sans', sans-serif; font-size: 22px; background-color: #ecf0f1;\">M.E.Moirin is the pen name for the author of The Girl Who Cheated the Wind, a Slavic fairytale and folklore fantasy (previously published as Threadbound). When not writing, her day job involves working in research. She's lived here and there, which explains her awkward silence when she's asked where she's from. She's now made the UK her home. She is obsessed with her cat, books, coffee and matcha, and loves the cosiness of autumn.&nbsp;<\/span><\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"Z0087","name":["Valerie Estelle Frankel"],"sortname":"Frankel Valerie Estelle","prog":["125","149","166"],"bio":"<p><span style=\"color: #373e3e; font-family: 'Amazon Ember', Ember, amazon_ember, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif; font-size: 15px; white-space: pre-wrap; background-color: #ffffff;\">Valerie Estelle Frankel has won a Dream Realm Award, an Indie Excellence Award, and a USA Book News National Best Book Award for her Henry Potty parodies. She's the author of over 100 books on pop culture, including Hunting for Meaning in The Mandalorian; The Villain&rsquo;s Journey; Star Wars Meets the Eras of Feminism; and Who Tells Your Story? History, Pop Culture, and Hidden Meanings in the Musical Phenomenon Hamilton. Many of her books focus on gender in fiction, from her heroine's journey guides From Girl to Goddess and Buffy and the Heroine's Journey to The Trans Hero's Journey and The Many Faces of Katniss Everdeen. Her Chelm for the Holidays (2019) was a PJ Library book, and now she&rsquo;s the editor of Jewish Science Fiction and Fantasy, publishing an academic series for Bloomsbury\/Lexington Press. Once a lecturer at San Jose State University, she now teaches at Mission College and San Jose City College and speaks often at conferences. Come explore her research at vefrankel.com.<\/span><\/p>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/pp875508cfa2abc656809cd6c01cd008ca.png"}},{"id":"Z0088","name":["Gerald T. Mc Evoy"],"sortname":"Gerald T. Mc Evoy","prog":["75","88","106","110","113","165"],"bio":"<p>Gerry is an ancient feral Autistic who can be spotted roaming the coasts of Ireland if the moon is just right, the rest of the time he writea with occasional input from my girlfriends dog Coco.<\/p>\n<p>He's a genre hopping fiction writer, creates ttrpg scenarios, currently designs and writes with Fainting Goat Games mainly on the D.A.M.N.I.T TTRPG (New Edition in Febuary) He also writes articles on a wide range of subjects.He currently has a couple of books born of community flash fiction projects out on multiple platforms and is currentlyvworking on some new books over on Patreon, one is due for release in December.<\/p>\n<p>When not pounding out fiction to heavy game soundtracks while inhaling coffee he practices internal martial arts, voluntarily runs Cons.ie, site promoting Irish fandom conventions and tries to survive in a world set to randomly increase difficulty levels at the worst possible times.<\/p>","links":{"photo":"https:\/\/participants.octocon.com\/participant_photos\/pp023ee64735065c5ef2b09d3660b3f167.jpg"}}]